 "text": " Why doesn't one big file clog up the wires for everyone else? Because networks use packet switching. Instead of sending a huge file all at once, your computer chops the message into small numbered chunks called packets. Each packet carries a header with the source IP, the destination's IP, and its sequence number. Packet 1 of 3, packet 2 of 3, and so on. Watch our interactive packet tracer in the study guide. First, with all links healthy, packets 1, 2, and 3 travels across the fast primary route from router A to router B to the server. Now watch what happens when I click server primary link to stimulate a caught cable during transmissions. The router immediately detects the broken link and dynamically sends the remaining packets along the backup path through router C. Because taking different paths can cause packet 3 to arrive before packet 2, the receiving computer uses the sequence numbers in the headers to put the package back into exact order 1, 2, 3 before showing you the message.",
